The '''leader of the National Party''' is the most-senior elected politician within the [[New Zealand National Party]]. Under the constitution of the party, they are required to be a member of the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]].

The National Party has found itself either in [[List of New Zealand governments|government]] (alone or with the support of other parties) or in [[Official Opposition (New Zealand)|opposition]] to Labour-led governments. Consequently, the leader of the National Party usually assumes the role of either the [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|prime minister]] or [[Leader of the Opposition (New Zealand)|leader of the Opposition]].

The current leader of the National Party [[2021 New Zealand National Party leadership election|since 30 November 2021]] is [[Christopher Luxon]].

==Selection==
Following a general election, or when a vacancy arises, the Parliamentary Section of the National Party (also called the [[Caucus]]) elects a leader of the Parliamentary Section (that is, the [[parliamentary leader]]). After receiving approval by the Board of Directors (the governing body of the party), the leader of the Parliamentary Section becomes the leader of the party.<ref name="Constitution">{{cite web |title=Constitution and Rules of the New Zealand National Party |edition=26th |date=2019 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/Party-rules/national-party-rules-and-constitution.pdf |publisher=New Zealand National Party |via=New Zealand Electoral Commission |accessdate=19 January 2020}}</ref>

==Role==
The leader organises the business of the party in [[New Zealand Parliament|Parliament]] and represents the party to the general public. Within the party organisation, they must ensure political consensus; the constitution of the National Party states that the leader has "the right to attend any Party meeting or committee meeting and shall be an ex officio member of the Board".<ref name="Constitution"/>

The leader becomes the National Party's candidate for [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|prime minister]] in the run-up to an [[elections in New Zealand|election]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sheppard |first1=Nicholas |title=Will Christopher Luxon be New Zealand’s prime minister? |url=https://www.spectator.co.uk/article/will-christopher-luxon-be-new-zealands-prime-minister/ |access-date=23 September 2023 |work=The Spectator |date=22 January 2023}}</ref> and they invariably take office as prime minister when the National Party forms a government. In [[1949 New Zealand general election|1949]], party leader [[Sidney Holland]] became the first prime minister from the National Party.<ref>{{cite web|title=Sidney Holland|url=https://nzhistory.govt.nz/media/photo/pm-sidney-holland|website=nzhistory.govt.nz|publisher=Ministry for Culture and Heritage|accessdate=22 April 2018|language=en}}</ref>

==List of leaders==
Of the fifteen people to officially hold the leadership, nine have served as prime minister.

